# hw | Quantum hardware | 🔧
> There are many ways to build a qubit. Each platform trades speed, quality, connectivity and how easy it is to scale.
" For a successful technology, reality must take precedence over public relations, for nature cannot be fooled. | Richard Feynman, Challenger accident report (1986)
## Spotlight: Google's Willow chip (December 2024)
H <div class="spot"><div class="spot-h">⭐ Spotlight · Dec 2024</div><b>Willow</b> is a 105-qubit superconducting chip from Google Quantum AI.<ul><li><b>Errors went down as the code got bigger.</b> Growing the error-correcting code from size 3 to 5 to 7 roughly halved the error rate each step (a factor of about 2.14). This "below threshold" behaviour is what error correction needs to scale.</li><li><b>The protected qubit outlived its parts.</b> The logical qubit kept its information about 2.4 times longer than the best single physical qubit on the chip.</li><li><b>A record sampling task.</b> Google says Willow finished a random-circuit sampling task in about 5 minutes that would take a top supercomputer about 10<sup>25</sup> years. This task is a benchmark, not a useful calculation, and such estimates depend on the best classical methods known.</li></ul><small>Sources: Google Quantum AI, "Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold", Nature (published 9 Dec 2024); Willow spec sheet (Dec 2024).</small></div>
Why it matters, simply: before Willow, adding more qubits for error correction often added more errors too. Willow showed the opposite trend: bigger codes, fewer errors. It is still far from a useful, large machine.
## Superconducting qubits (transmons)
Tiny circuits of superconducting metal with Josephson junctions, acting like artificial atoms. They are controlled with microwave pulses and have very fast gates (tens of nanoseconds). They need dilution refrigerators near 10–20 millikelvin and have shorter coherence times than atoms.
~ Like tiny electrical guitar strings that ring at a set note, played by microwave "plucks".
x superconducting means room temperature. They need to be colder than outer space.
A transmon is a tiny electrical circuit made of superconducting metal and a "Josephson junction", which makes its energy levels uneven. The lowest two levels are used as $|0\rangle$ and $|1\rangle$. Gates are microwave pulses lasting tens of nanoseconds. The chips must be cooled to about 10–20 millikelvin, colder than outer space.
$$\Delta E = h f_{01},\qquad f_{01}\sim 4\text{–}8\ \text{GHz}$$
## Trapped ions
Individual charged atoms held by electric fields in a vacuum and controlled with lasers. They have very long coherence and some of the highest gate fidelities, and any ion can interact with any other in a chain. Gates are slower, and scaling to many ions is a challenge.
~ Atoms floating in an electric "bowl", poked gently with laser beams.
x ions are slow so they lose. Their high accuracy can make up for slower gates.
Each qubit is a single charged atom held in place by electric fields in a vacuum. Two internal energy levels store the qubit. Lasers or microwaves do the gates. All the ions are identical by nature, and they can interact through their shared motion, so connectivity is excellent. The trade-off is slower gates than superconducting chips.
k Every ion of the same element is identical, so there is no manufacturing variation.
k Gates are typically microseconds long, slower than superconducting nanosecond gates.
## Photonic qubits
Information is stored in particles of light (polarisation, path, or time bins). Photons travel easily and barely decohere, making them natural for communication. The hard part is making photons interact; approaches use measurement-based schemes and fusion.
~ Light flashes carrying messages through a fibre, like a very special fibre-optic internet.
x photonic computers need no cooling at all. The photons don't, but the best detectors often do.
Photons (particles of light) can carry a qubit in their polarisation, path or arrival time. They barely interact with their surroundings, so they keep coherence well and travel far in optical fibre. The hard part is making two photons interact for two-qubit gates, so many designs use measurement and clever "fusion" of small entangled states.
k Natural fit for communication: photons travel long distances in fibre.
k Photon loss is the main error to fight.
## Neutral atoms
Uncharged atoms held in grids of laser "optical tweezers" that can be rearranged. Exciting atoms to Rydberg states makes them interact for 2-qubit gates. They can form large arrays and are flexible in layout.
~ Atoms held by laser "tweezers", arranged like pieces on a board that you can move around.
x they don't interact, so they can't make gates. Rydberg excitation makes them interact strongly.
Uncharged atoms are held by focused laser beams called "optical tweezers", arranged in 2D or 3D patterns. To entangle them, atoms are briefly excited to large "Rydberg" states, where they strongly feel each other. Atoms can even be moved around during a computation, which gives flexible connectivity.
k Hundreds of atoms can be trapped and rearranged in regular arrays.
k Two-qubit gates use the Rydberg blockade effect.
## Spin qubits
Use the spin of single electrons (or nuclei) in silicon or quantum dots. They are extremely small and could use existing chip factories, which helps scaling. Making them uniform and controlling them precisely is still being worked on.
~ The tiny "spin" of an electron acts like a mini compass needle that points up or down.
x they can use any normal chip. They need very pure materials and special cold electronics.
The qubit is the spin of a single electron (or hole) trapped in a tiny "quantum dot" in silicon or similar materials. Because they are made like normal computer chips, they could one day use existing chip factories. They are very small, which is great for packing millions together, but also makes wiring and control hard.
k Spin up / spin down plays the role of |0⟩ / |1⟩.
k Compatible with semiconductor manufacturing.
## Topological qubits (Majorana)
The idea: store information non-locally in exotic quasiparticles (like Majorana zero modes), so local noise can't easily damage it. Braiding them would perform gates. This approach is still at an early, experimental stage.
~ Like a knot in a rope: wiggling the rope doesn't undo the knot. Information stored in the knot is safe from small shakes.
x topological qubits are already working at scale. They are still in early research.
The idea is to store information in the overall "shape" of a system, not in one small place. Local noise can't easily change that shape, so errors should be naturally rare. Majorana zero modes are one proposed way to do this. Building and confirming them is very hard, and this approach is still at an early research stage.
k Protection comes from non-local storage of information.
k Gates come from "braiding" exotic quasiparticles around each other.
## NV centres in diamond
A nitrogen atom next to a missing carbon atom in diamond traps an electron spin that can be controlled with light and microwaves, even at room temperature. They are mainly used for quantum sensing and network experiments.
~ A tiny flaw in a diamond acts like a super-sensitive compass.
x diamonds make good big quantum computers. NV centres are mostly used for sensing and networking.
An NV centre is a defect in diamond: a nitrogen atom next to a missing carbon atom. Its electron spin can be controlled with lasers and microwaves, even at room temperature. NV centres are especially good as quantum sensors, measuring tiny magnetic fields, for example inside living cells.
k Works at room temperature, unlike most qubits.
k Leading platform for nanoscale magnetic sensing.
## Control, readout and cryogenics
Qubits are steered by carefully shaped microwave or laser pulses and read out with resonators, fluorescence or charge sensors. Many platforms need extreme cold or ultra-high vacuum. Wiring and control electronics become a major engineering problem as qubit counts grow.
~ Like a huge orchestra: every instrument needs its own perfectly timed signal and a microphone to hear it.
x the qubit chip is the hard part. Wiring, cooling and control electronics are just as big a challenge.
A quantum computer is mostly classical equipment: fridges, lasers, microwave generators, fast electronics and software. Each qubit needs control lines, and the heat and space they take is a major barrier to scaling. Readout must be fast and accurate, because a wrong measurement is just another error.
k Dilution refrigerators reach about 10 mK for superconducting and spin qubits.
k Fast, accurate readout is essential for error correction.
## Performance metrics
Coherence time ($T_1$, $T_2$) is how long qubits stay quantum. Gate fidelity is how accurately gates work (for example 99.9% means about 1 error per 1000 gates). Quantum volume is a single-number benchmark combining qubit count, connectivity and error rates.
~ Like judging a car by top speed, fuel use and reliability, not just one number.
x more qubits always means a better computer. Quality and connectivity matter just as much.
No single number tells you how good a quantum computer is. You need to know how many qubits there are, how accurate the gates and measurements are, how long the qubits last (T1, T2), how they are connected, and how fast operations run. Error rates per gate are often the most important.
$$\text{error rate}=1-F,\qquad \text{useful depth}\sim\frac{1}{\text{error rate}}$$
## Benchmarking: randomized benchmarking and quantum volume
How do you measure a gate's quality? **Randomized benchmarking** runs long random sequences of Clifford gates that should multiply to "do nothing", then checks how often the qubit survives. The survival drops as the sequences get longer, and the speed of that drop gives the average error per gate.
**Quantum volume** and similar whole-system tests run random circuits of growing size to see the biggest "square" circuit the machine can run well. They reflect qubit count, error rates and connectivity together.
$$P_{\text{survive}}(m)\approx A\,p^{m}+B,\qquad r=\frac{(1-p)(d-1)}{d}\ \ (d=2\text{ for one qubit})$$
~ Like testing a runner by timing laps of increasing length, rather than one short sprint.
x a higher qubit count alone means a better machine. Benchmarks show that quality and connectivity matter just as much.
## Annealing vs gate-based machines
There are two main styles of quantum computer.
**Gate-based** (also called "universal") machines run circuits of gates, like the ones in this guide. In principle they can run any quantum algorithm, including Shor and Grover. IBM, Google, Quantinuum, IonQ and others build these.
**Quantum annealers** do one job: they look for low-energy answers to optimisation problems. You write your problem as an "energy landscape", the machine starts in an easy state and slowly changes to your problem, hoping to end in the lowest valley. D-Wave builds these with thousands of qubits.
Simple comparison: annealers have more qubits but can only do this one kind of task, and it is still debated how much faster they are than good classical methods. Gate-based machines are more flexible but are harder to build at large size.
~ An annealer is like shaking a tray of marbles so they settle into the lowest holes. A gate-based computer is like a programmable robot that can follow any recipe you write.
## What qubits are made of: materials and chips
A qubit is not a special particle you buy. It is any small physical system with two states we can control. Different companies build them from very different materials.
**Superconducting qubits** (IBM, Google and others). A thin metal film, usually **aluminium**, **niobium** or **tantalum**, is patterned on a **silicon** or **sapphire** wafer, just like a computer chip. The key part is a **Josephson junction**: two layers of aluminium with an insulating oxide only about 1–2 nanometres thick between them. The chip sits in a copper box inside a **dilution refrigerator** at about 10 millikelvin (−273.14 °C), so the metal superconducts and heat does not disturb the qubit.
