d" font-size="12">Josephson junction</text> <rect x="330" y="175" width="290" height="135" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#fbbf24"/><text x="345" y="197" fill="#fcd34d" font-weight="700">Zoom: the junction</text> <rect x="355" y="212" width="240" height="22" fill="#22d3ee"/><text x="365" y="228" fill="#020611" font-size="12" font-weight="700">aluminium (superconductor)</text> <rect x="355" y="236" width="240" height="10" fill="#f8fafc"/><text x="600" y="245" fill="#b7c8e6" font-size="11" text-anchor="end"> </text> <rect x="355" y="248" width="240" height="22" fill="#22d3ee"/><text x="365" y="264" fill="#020611" font-size="12" font-weight="700">aluminium (superconductor)</text> <text x="355" y="292" fill="#b7c8e6" font-size="12">white layer: aluminium oxide, only ~1–2 nm thick</text></g></svg><figcaption>Simplified, not to scale. A superconducting qubit chip from the fridge down to its junction.</figcaption></figure>
**Silicon spin qubits** (for example Intel and Diraq). One electron is trapped in a tiny device similar to a transistor, made from silicon (often purified silicon-28) or silicon–germanium. The qubit is the electron's **spin** (up or down). These also need very cold fridges, though often slightly warmer (around 1 kelvin is being explored).
**Trapped ions** (Quantinuum, IonQ). Single charged atoms, such as ytterbium, calcium or barium, float in a vacuum held by electric fields from a chip-like electrode trap. Lasers or microwaves control them.
**Neutral atoms** (QuEra, Pasqal, Atom Computing). Uncharged atoms, such as rubidium, caesium or strontium, are held in rows by focused laser beams called **optical tweezers**.
**Photonic qubits** (PsiQuantum, Xanadu). The qubit is a particle of light. Chips made of silicon or silicon nitride guide the light through tiny channels called waveguides.
**NV centres in diamond.** A nitrogen atom next to a missing carbon atom in diamond holds a spin that can be a qubit, even at room temperature. Today these are used more for super-sensitive sensors than for big computers.
x "Qubits are made of silicon and nickel." Silicon is right: it is used as the chip base for superconducting qubits and for spin qubits. Nickel is not a standard qubit material (it is magnetic, which tends to disturb qubits). The common superconducting metals are **aluminium, niobium and tantalum**.
k Superconducting qubit films: aluminium, niobium or tantalum on silicon or sapphire.
k Josephson junction: aluminium / aluminium oxide / aluminium.
k Operating temperature of superconducting chips: about 10 mK, in a dilution refrigerator.
## Topological qubits (Majorana): promising but unproven
**The idea.** Most qubits store information in one small, fragile thing, so any small disturbance can cause an error. A **topological qubit** would store information in the overall pattern (the **topology**) of a material, spread across space. A small local bump cannot change a global pattern, so the qubit would be naturally protected from many errors.
**Majorana zero modes.** In theory, special states called **Majorana zero modes** can appear at the two ends of a very thin wire made of a semiconductor (such as indium arsenide) touching a superconductor (such as aluminium), in a magnetic field. One qubit is stored jointly in a pair of these end states, so disturbing just one end does not reveal or destroy the information.
**Anyons and braiding.** Majorana zero modes are an example of **anyons**, particle-like states that can only exist in 2D or 1D systems. Moving anyons around each other, called **braiding**, changes the qubit in a way that depends only on the pattern of the moves, not on small wobbles. That is why braiding could give very reliable gates. (Braiding Majoranas alone cannot do every gate, so extra steps would still be needed.)
~ Think of a knot in a rope. Shaking the rope does not undo the knot; only a big, deliberate change can. Topological qubits aim to store information like a knot.
**Microsoft Majorana 1 (February 2025).** Microsoft announced a chip called **Majorana 1** and said it holds 8 topological qubits. It published a paper in *Nature* the same day. That paper showed fast, accurate measurements in indium arsenide–aluminium nanowire devices, but Nature's editors added a note that the results **do not prove** Majorana zero modes exist in the devices.
**The debate.** Some physicists (for example Henry Legg) argued that the test Microsoft used to find the "topological phase" can give false positives. Microsoft says it stands by its results and presented more data at the APS March Meeting in 2025. As of 2026, many experts say a working topological qubit has not yet been clearly demonstrated. It is an important open question.
x "Microsoft has proven topological qubits work." Not yet. The claim is promising but still disputed.
## The DiVincenzo criteria: what makes a good qubit?
In **2000**, David DiVincenzo listed what any quantum computer needs. In simple words:
1. Qubits you can **scale** up in number. 2. You can **reset** them to a starting state. 3. They stay quantum long enough (long **coherence**). 4. A complete set of **gates**. 5. You can **measure** each qubit. (For networks, two more: turn stationary qubits into flying ones, such as photons, and send them reliably.)
H <div class="dv6" id="dv6"><label>Compare a platform <select class="pf"><option value="sc">Superconducting</option><option value="ion">Trapped ions</option><option value="atom">Neutral atoms</option><option value="spin">Silicon spins</option><option value="ph">Photons</option></select></label><div class="out6" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
## Continuous-variable and bosonic qubits
Instead of two levels, some designs store information in a **light-like wave** with many levels, such as the light inside a superconducting box (a "cavity") or a pulse of laser light. These are **continuous-variable** or **bosonic** qubits.
**Cat qubits** use two opposite wave states (named after Schrödinger's cat). They naturally resist bit flips, leaving mostly phase flips to correct; Alice & Bob and AWS (with its Ocelot chip, 2025) work on these. **GKP codes** store a qubit in a grid pattern of wave states. **Photonic** companies such as Xanadu use squeezed light.
## Isotopically pure silicon-28
Natural silicon contains about 4.7% silicon-29, whose atomic nuclei act like tiny magnets that disturb electron spin qubits. Purifying silicon to almost pure **silicon-28** (which has no nuclear spin) makes spin qubits keep their state much longer.
## Lithography and nanofabrication
Qubit chips are drawn with **lithography**: light (photolithography) for bigger features, and an electron beam (e-beam lithography) for the tiniest parts, like Josephson junctions. Spin-qubit chips can be made in the same kind of factories that make normal computer chips, which could help them scale.
## Distributed and modular quantum computers
One chip can only hold so many qubits. A **modular** design links several smaller quantum processors, usually with photons, so they work as one bigger machine. Entanglement is shared between modules and then used to run gates across them (via teleportation).
Many companies (for example IBM and IonQ) and university labs are working on this, because it may be easier than building one giant chip.
! There's no clear winner yet. Superconducting is fast, ions are precise, neutral atoms scale in arrays, photons travel, and spins are tiny.
